{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_myiqho45aoinvn2icnwon7f7a2atmtri6pz5mjcuuoapau3djzrq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01BRJjnZ9g5B1TDBpd2SaGeZ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-translate-YrWmIc/translated_rust/src/lib.rs","content":"// Translation of c_src/src/lib.c\n//\n// The C source is a single-file port of stb_ds plus an `arr_del(int num)` entry\n// point. The CMake target produces a SHARED library — there is no main() and\n// no I/O. We only need to faithfully model the externally observable behavior\n// of `arr_del`, which is: modify a local dynamic array and free it. There is\n// no output and no return value, so the function effectively does nothing\n// observable from outside.\n//\n// We reproduce the same sequence of array operations using a safe Vec<i32>\n// to preserve the exact logical behavior the C version performs (push four\n// elements, delete index i / swap-delete index i, free; repeat for i in 0..4).\n//\n// NOTE: For i == 4 the C original would fail STBDS_ASSERT in debug builds\n// (length 4, deleting index 4 — the loop only runs for i in [0, 4), so the\n// max valid value of i is 3). The Rust loop matches the same iteration count.\n\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n/// Equivalent of the C `arr_del(int num)` exposed by lib.h.\npub fn arr_del(num: i32) {\n    for i in 0..4usize {\n        // arrpush(arr,num); arrpush(arr,2); arrpush(arr,3); arrpush(arr,4);\n        let mut arr: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n        arr.push(num);\n        arr.push(2);\n        arr.push(3);\n        arr.push(4);\n        // arrdel(arr,i);\n        arr.remove(i);\n        // arrfree(arr);\n        drop(arr);\n\n        // arrpush(arr,num); arrpush(arr,2); arrpush(arr,3); arrpush(arr,4);\n        let mut arr: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n        arr.push(num);\n        arr.push(2);\n        arr.push(3);\n        arr.push(4);\n        // arrdelswap(arr,i): replace arr[i] with last element, then pop.\n        let last = arr.pop().expect(\"arrdelswap on empty array\");\n        if i < arr.len() {\n            arr[i] = last;\n        } else {\n            // Mirrors the C macro behavior: assigns last to arr[i], then\n            // decrements length. We've already popped `last`; if i == len\n            // (after pop), there's nothing further to do that's observable.\n        }\n        // arrfree(arr);\n        drop(arr);\n    }\n}\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":36290,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3cf1a7c1-1441-4a70-b5ad-f8a106796b4b","uuid":"0ce35242-0479-40ce-960f-c163449b80d4"}
